# Never upload these, whatever happens to be sitting in dist/.
#
# `npm run sounds` extracts audio from a local Minecraft install into
# public/sounds/, vite copies public/ into dist/ wholesale, and from there a
# single `wrangler deploy` would publish Mojang's audio from Evan's domain.
# That is redistribution, and public/sounds/ is gitignored precisely to stop
# it -- but gitignore only guards the repo, not the deploy.
#
# There IS a redistributable set now (sounds-src/free, CC0/CC-BY/CC-BY-SA), so
# audio is no longer blanket-refused -- `build:deploy` runs `npm run sounds`,
# which writes the free source and stamps dist/sounds/.source with its name.
#
# The lock moved rather than disappearing: the deploy workflow asserts that
# marker reads "free". That is strictly stronger than refusing a directory,
# because it fails on a Mojang-sourced build AND on a silent one, where the
# old rule quietly passed for both.

# Terrain, for a reason that is NOT settled the way the two above are.
#
# public/terrain/ holds a patch of real Minecraft terrain -- the output of
# Mojang's world generator, not their artwork. Whether that is theirs to
# withhold is an open question (docs/DEPLOYMENT.md), and this is the
# conservative default while it stays open. gitignore alone does not cover
# it: vite copies public/ into dist/ wholesale, so the data reaches the
# deploy artifact without ever being committed. Verified -- it did.
terrain/
